# Version Control with Git: Tracking Every Change

> Every serious codebase is a history, not a file. Git records who
> changed what, when, and why — letting you experiment fearlessly,
> collaborate safely, and travel back in time when tomorrow's code
> breaks yesterday's work.

## 1. Why Version Control Matters

Before version control, teams emailed zips (`final_v2_REAL.zip`) and
lived in fear of overwriting a colleague's work. A **version control
system (VCS)** stores a project as a chain of snapshots: each
**commit** records the exact state of every file, plus who changed it
and why.

| Pain without a VCS | Git's answer |
| --- | --- |
| "Which version is current?" | Every change is a hashed, named commit |
| A change broke working code | `git restore` / `git revert` rewind safely |
| Two people edit the same file | Branches merge automatically where possible |
| Why was this line written? | `git log` and `git blame` give the history |

Git is **distributed**: every clone holds the full history, so you
can commit on a train with no internet and sync later.

## 2. Getting Started: init, config, clone

```bash
git config --global user.name "Ada Lovelace"
git config --global user.email "ada@example.com"

git init                       # start tracking the current folder
git clone https://github.com/org/project.git   # copy an existing repo
```

`git init` creates a hidden `.git/` directory — the database that
stores all history. Deleting it turns your project back into a plain
folder; nothing in your working files is lost.

[info]
`git config --global init.defaultBranch main` names your default
branch `main` (the modern convention) instead of the older `master`.
[/info]

## 3. The Three Areas: status, add, commit

Git has three places your work can live, and every everyday command
moves files between them.

@diagram tree id="git-three-areas" layout="tree" aria="Git areas: working directory, staging area, local repository, and remote repository"
  "Git areas" -> "Working directory"
  "Git areas" -> "Staging area"
  "Git areas" -> "Local repository"
  "Git areas" -> "Remote repository"
@/diagram

```bash
git status                     # what changed, what is staged
git add report.py              # stage one file (working -> staging)
git add .                      # stage everything in the folder
git commit -m "Add report generator"   # snapshot (staging -> history)
```

The **staging area** (index) is Git's superpower: it lets you commit
*selected* changes — one logical change per commit, even if you
edited five files for three different reasons.

@chart bar title="Commands a developer runs on a typical day (illustrative counts)" labels:
status
add
commit
push
pull
log

data:
14
9
9
7
7
5
@/chart

[definition]
A **commit** is an immutable snapshot of the staged files, plus the
author, timestamp, message, and a pointer to the previous commit.
Its hash (a short SHA-1 string) is its name forever.
[/definition]

## 4. Reading History — and Undoing Mistakes

```bash
git log --oneline              # compact history
git log --oneline --graph --all   # history with a branch picture
git diff                       # unstaged changes, line by line
git restore report.py          # discard uncommitted edits to a file
git revert a1b2c3d             # new commit that undoes an old one
```

[important]
Prefer `revert` over rewriting published history: it makes a *new*
commit that cancels an old one, so teammates' histories stay
consistent. `git reset --hard` rewrites your local past — never run
it on commits that others already have.
[/important]

## 5. Branching: Cheap Experiments

A **branch** is just a movable label pointing at a commit — creating
one costs nothing, which is why Git users branch for every feature,
fix, or even a risky idea they may abandon.

```bash
git switch -c feature/login    # create and move to a new branch
# ... edit, add, commit, repeat ...
git switch main                # return to the main line
git merge feature/login        # bring the feature in
git branch -d feature/login    # tidy up
```

@diagram flowchart id="branch-workflow" layout="hierarchical" aria="Feature branch workflow from task to merged and pushed code"
node start label="Pick a task" shape="pill"
node branch label="git switch -c feature/x" shape="round-rect"
node edit label="Edit, add, commit repeatedly" shape="round-rect"
node clean label="Tests pass?" shape="diamond"
node merge label="git switch main; git merge feature/x" shape="round-rect"
node push label="git push and open a pull request" shape="pill"
node fix label="Fix code, commit again" shape="round-rect"

start -> branch
branch -> edit
edit -> clean
clean -> fix label="no"
fix -> edit
clean -> merge label="yes"
merge -> push
@/diagram

## 6. Merging and Merge Conflicts: A Walkthrough

Merging stitches two lines of history together. Git merges
automatically when changes touch different lines — but when both
branches edit the **same lines differently**, it stops and asks you
to decide. The conflicted file shows both versions:

```text
<<<<<<< HEAD
Ship the report as PDF.
=======
Ship the report as HTML.
>>>>>>> feature/format
```

Resolution is editing the file to the text you actually want,
removing the `<<<<<<<`, `=======`, and `>>>>>>>` markers, then
staging:

```bash
git add report.txt
git commit                     # completes the merge
```

[tip]
Conflict markers mark *both* versions — HEAD is your current branch,
and the label after `>>>>>>>` is the incoming one. Read both sides,
keep what makes sense (sometimes both), and never keep the markers
themselves: a file containing `<<<<<<<` will not compile or run.
[/tip]

## 7. Remotes: push, pull, fetch — and Git vs GitHub

A **remote** is another copy of the repository, usually a server.
`origin` is the conventional name for the one you cloned from.

```bash
git remote add origin https://github.com/me/project.git
git push -u origin main        # upload commits, set upstream
git pull                       # fetch remote commits + merge them
git fetch                      # download only; inspect before merging
```

[warning]
**Git is not GitHub.** Git is the open-source version control tool
that runs on your machine; GitHub is a commercial hosting service
that stores repositories online and adds issues, pull requests, and
code review. `git clone` works against any Git server — GitLab and
self-hosted servers included.
[/warning]

## 8. .gitignore and Commit Message Discipline

Commit what is *yours* — source and configuration — not what the
machine generated. A `.gitignore` file at the repo root lists
patterns Git should never track:

```text
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
node_modules/
dist/
```

Good commit messages complete the sentence "This commit will…":

| Message | Verdict |
| --- | --- |
| `Fix bug` | Useless — which bug? how? |
| `Fix off-by-one in loop bounds of parse_rows()` | Specific and searchable |
| Fifty unrelated files, one message | One commit should be one logical change |

[tip]
Write the message for the teammate reading `git log` in six months —
often that teammate is you, and the subject line is all they will
see. Keep the subject under about 50 characters, written in the
imperative, and reserve the body for the *why*, not the what.
[/tip]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Commit | Immutable snapshot + author + message + parent |
| Three areas | Working directory -> staging (`add`) -> history (`commit`) |
| Branch | Movable label on a commit — create one per feature |
| Merge conflict | Same lines changed twice: edit, unmark, add, commit |
| Remote | `origin` on GitHub etc.; `push` up, `pull` down |
| Git vs GitHub | Tool vs hosting service — never confuse them |
